2026-06-04 15:50:08 [http-nio-8080-exec-2] DEBUG c.d.w.s.e.WikipediaIngestionService - Wikipedia API response body: {"batchcomplete":"","continue":{"grncontinue":"0.647969425491|0.647970463152|51137012|0","continue":"grncontinue||"},"query":{"pages":{"7863599":{"pageid":7863599,"ns":0,"title":"Peter James Spielmann","extract":"Peter James Spielmann (born 1952) is a veteran reporter in the foreign service of The Associated Press, and is an editor and supervisor on AP's North America desk. He taught at Columbia University's Graduate School of Journalism from 1989–93, and from 2001–07.\nSpielmann is a member of the editorial board of the quarterly Journal of Human Rights He has been a public speaker on human rights and journalism issues at many venues, including the Nieman Foundation for Journalism at Harvard; New York University; Fordham University School of Law; Purchase College; and the Foreign Press Center in New York City."},"30617466":{"pageid":30617466,"ns":0,"title":"Venus (Belgian band)","extract":"Venus was a band created in 1997 by five artists hailing from Brussels and Belgium. The original goal of the band was to create an original sound that expressed different emotions through several instruments including guitar, violin, double bass, organ, percussion, synth, and a host of others. Also using the scenography talents of former member Patric Carpentier to create visual emotion through their live performances."},"13449019":{"pageid":13449019,"ns":0,"title":"The Lady of the Lake (poem)","extract":"The Lady of the Lake is a narrative poem by Walter Scott, first published in 1810. Set in the Trossachs region of Scotland, it is composed of six cantos, each of which concerns the action of a single day. There are voluminous antiquarian notes.","thumbnail":{"source":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/0030203d.jpg/500px-0030203d.jpg","width":500,"height":780},"pageimage":"0030203d.jpg"},"46850083":{"pageid":46850083,"ns":0,"title":"2015 UniCredit Czech Open – Singles","extract":"Jiří Veselý was the defending champion and successfully defended his title, defeating Laslo Djere in the final, 6–4, 6–2."},"12759047":{"pageid":12759047,"ns":0,"title":"Zeb Taia","extract":"Zeb Taia (born 11 October 1984) is a former professional rugby league footballer who played as a second-row forward for both New Zealand and the Cook Islands at international level.\nHe played for the Parramatta Eels, Newcastle Knights and the Gold Coast Titans in the NRL, and the Catalans Dragons and St Helens in the Super League. Taia has also played for the Exiles, and earlier in his career he played as a lock, centre and prop.","thumbnail":{"source":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Zeb_Taia.jpg/500px-Zeb_Taia.jpg","width":500,"height":768},"pageimage":"Zeb_Taia.jpg"},"21464739":{"pageid":21464739,"ns":0,"title":"Rich Woman","extract":"\"Rich Woman\" is a song written by Dorothy LaBostrie and McKinley \"Li'l\" Millet, who recorded it in 1955, and was most notably recorded by Robert Plant and Alison Krauss on their 2007 album Raising Sand."},"82056120":{"pageid":82056120,"ns":0,"title":"Lesley (Dave song)","extract":"\"Lesley\" is a song by British rapper Dave, featuring American singer-songwriter Ruelle, released on 8 March 2019 by Neighbourhood Recordings as the ninth cut of Dave's debut studio album, Psychodrama. Over eleven minutes long, it was produced by Dave's mentor, Fraser T. Smith and marks Dave's longest released song."},"7075063":{"pageid":7075063,"ns":0,"title":"Earnock","extract":"Earnock was an ancient estate in an area south of Hamilton in Lanarkshire, Scotland. It extended from the western side of Strathaven Road to the western extremity of the Parish of Hamilton."},"5747021":{"pageid":5747021,"ns":0,"title":"The Aquitania","extract":"The Aquitania is a luxury, 82-unit cooperative apartment building in the Margate Park neighborhood of the Uptown community of Chicago, Illinois. It is officially designated on the National Register of Historic Places by The United States Department of the Interior.  The name was likely a nod to the Cunard transatlantic express ocean liner, RMS Aquitania, which entered service in 1914 and was one of the most popular ships on the North Atlantic in the 1920s, sailing until 1949.","thumbnail":{"source":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Aquitania%2C_Chicago.jpg/500px-Aquitania%2C_Chicago.jpg","width":500,"height":667},"pageimage":"Aquitania,_Chicago.jpg"},"70705580":{"pageid":70705580,"ns":0,"title":"Robert Stainton","extract":"Robert Stainton (born 1964) is a Canadian philosopher/linguist and Distinguished University Professor at the University of Western Ontario. He is known for his works on the philosophy of language, cognitive science/philosophy of the mind, analytic metaphysics/philosophical logic, semantics and pragmatics. He is currently leading a Social Sciences and Humanities Research Council of Canada (SSHRC) Insight Grant-funded project on The Metaphysics and Epistemology of Languages with Christopher Viger.","thumbnail":{"source":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/RobertStainton.jpg/500px-RobertStainton.jpg","width":500,"height":667},"pageimage":"RobertStainton.jpg"}}}}
